[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Routes, Catch-all Segments, and Optional Catch-all in Next.js"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Routing is a core feature of Next.js. Beyond static pages, modern web applications frequently require dynamic paths to render content based on parameters like user IDs, blog slugs, or multi-level category filters. This tutorial explores standard dynamic routes, catch-all segments, and optional catch-all segments using the App Router."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Standard Dynamic Routes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you do not know the exact segment names ahead of time and want to create routes from dynamic data, you can use a Dynamic Segment. A dynamic segment can be created by wrapping a folder name in square brackets: [folderName]."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, to create a dynamic route for blog posts, structure your directory like this: app/blog/[slug]/page.js."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"// app/blog/[slug]/page.js

export default async function BlogPost({ params }) {
  const { slug } = await params;

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>
      <p>This is a dynamic route rendering content for the slug above.</p>
    </main>
  );
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Catch-all Segments"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic Routes can be extended to catch all subsequent segments by adding an ellipsis inside the brackets: [...folderName]. This matches routes with multiple segments, such as app/shop/[...slug]/page.js matching /shop/clothes/tops/t-shirts."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// app/shop/[...slug]/page.js

export default async function ShopCategory({ params }) {
  const { slug } = await params;

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Shop Path Segments</h1>
      <pre>{JSON.stringify(slug, null, 2)}</pre>
    </main>
  );
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, visiting /shop/clothes/tops/t-shirts will yield a slug array of ['clothes', 'tops', 't-shirts']."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Optional Catch-all Segments"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Catch-all segments can be made optional by including the parameter in double square brackets: [[...folderName]]. The key difference between catch-all and optional catch-all is that with the latter, the route without the parameter is also matched (e.g., /shop in addition to /shop/clothes/tops)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// app/shop/[[...slug]]/page.js

export default async function OptionalShop({ params }) {
  const resolvedParams = await params;
  const slug = resolvedParams.slug || [];

  if (slug.length === 0) {
    return <h1>Welcome to the main Shop page!</h1>;
  }

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Filtered Shop Page</h1>
      <p>Active filters: {slug.join(' > ')}</p>
    </main>
  );
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Route Matching Patterns"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[param]: Matches a single URL segment. Example: /blog/a matches slug = 'a'."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[...param]: Matches one or more URL segments. Example: /shop/a/b matches slug = ['a', 'b']."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[[...param]]: Matches zero, one, or more URL segments. Example: /shop matches slug = undefined."}]
